# {{lb|en|of a [[social network]] of equal partners}} Able to conduct business without using a [[middleman]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=71|magazine=w:The Economist|title=End of the peer show|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show|passage=Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of '''peer-to-peer''' lending comes close. This is an industry that brings together individual savers and lenders on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to lend.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|of a [[network]]}} Connected as [[equal]] [[partner]]s and able to [[share]] [[processing]], [[control]] and [[access]] to [[data]] and [[peripheral]]s; [[decentralized]].
#: {{syn|en|P2P}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Dinesh Verma|title=Legitimate Applications of Peer-to-Peer Networks|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9780471653790|page=98|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=k4P7gwcDrXEC&pg=PA98&dq=%22peer-to-peer%22&hl=&cd=11&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22peer-to-peer%22&f=false|text=With the '''peer-to-peer''' backup paradigm, each file is copied over to another peer in the system rather than to a central backup site.}}

